[{"id":"31121","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which five year plan of India came after the annual plans between 1966-1969?</span></p>","option_1":"Second","option_2":"Fourth","option_3":"Sixth","option_4":"Seventh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Fourth.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>After the three Annual Plans (1966-69), necessitated by the failure of the Third Plan and the wars of 1965 and drought, the Fourth Five Year Plan (1969-74) was launched, with 'growth with stability' and self-reliance as its objectives.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31122","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following was a major issue at the beginning of the first five year plan of India?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Influx of refugees</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Severe food shortage</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>At the launch of the First Five Year Plan (1951-56), India faced the huge challenge of resettling refugees from Partition as well as an acute food shortage, both of which shaped the Plan's priorities on agriculture, irrigation and rehabilitation. Both statements are correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31123","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the name of the devotional philosophy promoted by Shankardeva?</span></p>","option_1":"Dvaita Vedanta","option_2":"Advaita Vedanta","option_3":"Achintya Bheda Abheda","option_4":"Eka Sarana Nama Dharma","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Eka Sarana Nama Dharma.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Srimanta Shankardeva, the 15th-16th century Vaishnavite saint-reformer of Assam, propagated the monotheistic devotional philosophy known as 'Eka Sarana Nama Dharma' ('surrender to one God'), centred on devotion to Krishna through the recitation of his name.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31124","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What role do teeth play in the digestion of food?</span></p>","option_1":"They secrete enzymes","option_2":"They crush and grind the food into smaller particles","option_3":"They produce saliva","option_4":"They absorb nutrients from the food","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - They crush and grind the food into smaller particles.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Teeth perform mechanical digestion by cutting, tearing, crushing and grinding food into smaller particles, which increases the surface area for enzymes (secreted by salivary glands, not teeth) to act upon during chemical digestion.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31125","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Breakdown of pyruvate using oxygen takes place in the _____ in a cell.</span></p>","option_1":"cytoplasm","option_2":"lysosome","option_3":"mitochondria","option_4":"cell membrane","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - mitochondria.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Pyruvate produced during glycolysis (in the cytoplasm) enters the mitochondria, where, in the presence of oxygen, it undergoes aerobic respiration (via the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain) to release energy.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31126","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"834","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which Veda is river Sarasvati mentioned for the first time?</span></p>","option_1":"Sam Veda","option_2":"Yajur Veda","option_3":"Atharva Veda","option_4":"Rig Veda","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Rig Veda.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The river Sarasvati is first mentioned in the Rigveda, the oldest of the four Vedas, where it is glorified as a mighty and sacred river, and features prominently in the 'Nadistuti Sukta' (hymn in praise of rivers).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2